BLOCKING THE REBUILD
  [2026-09-11] Current-version scoping is in
  ~/docking/scratch/claude_plan_notes/db-preprocessing-plan.md: this rebuild uses
  prepwizard_BioLiP2 after a text-level repair pass (chain-ID + modres fixes below),
  already run -- the parent DB is
  /wynton/group/degradolab/skt/docking/databases/prepwizard_BioLiP2_repaired, identity
  0119d9ab2502371f..., and every later step must point at it. Waters/ions, the 20 A
  trim rerun and the orphan-N filter wait for the next parent DB (no s01/s02 rerun now).
  Items below are unchanged as the record for that rerun.
- THE SUBGRAPH ENUMERATOR. rebuild-notes.md section 2 decided to replace radius-ball
  fragments with all connected subgraphs, thresholded on support. No such code exists:
  `fragment_database_ligs.py` still enumerates balls (`--bond-radius`,
  `Frags.get_fragments`). This is the largest remaining piece and it blocks the whole
  data chain. Acceptance criteria (these were separate TODO bullets; they belong to the
  new enumerator, not to the old fragmenter):
    * Support threshold counts DISTINCT PARENT BIOUNITS. `--min-instances` counts a
      third unit today.
    * `ProcessPoolExecutor`, not `mp.Pool`/`imap` (`fragment_database_ligs.py:294-295`).
      The `finally: pool.terminate()` at :323-326 only fires on a parent-side exception;
      a SIGKILLed worker still hangs `imap` forever. `estimate_frag_cost.py:22-26,294-303`
      already has the target pattern with broken-pool handling.
    * Atomic output write (`.tmp` + `os.replace`); `output_results` (:352-354) is a plain
      open+dump today.
    * Stats logged even on exception: `report_stats` (:336) sits outside the try/finally
      that wraps the merge loop (:303-326).
    * `--force`/`--overwrite` instead of the bare `FileExistsError` at :240-242.
    * Whole-ring keys must not split across cyclic rotations of the cut path -- see the
      next bullet. Expected moot under canonical subgraph keys; VERIFY, don't assume.
- Whole-ring fragments get split across multiple keys (one per cyclic rotation of the
  cut path), fragmenting stats/mining across duplicate libraries. Dedup is mutual
  substructure matching between key query mols (`utils.py:499-508`, called from
  `fragment_database_ligs.py:485`), which collapses reversal but NOT rotation: a ring-cut
  submol is an open chain (`Frags.py:509-511`), and two rotations are non-isomorphic
  labeled paths. Measured on the production dict: 97 all-`r5` 5-atom keys fall into
  rotation classes, 16 of which hold >1 key. Worst case pyrrolidine `CCCCN` splits
  1061/554/171 ligands across 12 spellings; THF-type `CCCCO` splits 255/95/64. Doesn't
  apply to aromatic rings. Fix: canonicalize whole-ring keys over rotation+reversal
  before insertion, or get it for free from canonical subgraph keys.
- STALE RESOURCES, AND EVERY PROVENANCE GUARD PASSES ON THEM. `resources/database_frags_dict.pkl`
  (2026-09-06) and `resources/frag_cost_estimate.tsv` (2026-09-09) hold PRE-ANNOTATION
  SMARTS, e.g. `[O;!R]=[C;!R]([O;!R])[C;!R][O;!R]`, while `Frags.get_fragments` now emits
  `[O;!R;D1]=[C;!R;H0](...)`. Verified not interchangeable:
  `utils.fragment_keys_equivalent(old, new)` is False (a permuted new key against itself
  is True, so the check discriminates). The two artifacts are sha-consistent with EACH
  OTHER, so `frags_dict_sha256` passes, `select_fragments` does not raise, and a build
  would silently produce a library in the old vocabulary. The tsv also predates the
  `# pdb_db_identity` header, so the always-fatal identity check at
  `make_sge_scripts_for_frags.py:249-259` has nothing to compare and only warns.
  Both are regenerated by the enumerator + `estimate_frag_cost.py --pdb-dir
  .../prepwizard_BioLiP2_repaired`. Do not build until they are.
- Preprocessed DB has zero waters/monatomic ions (checked HOH/ZN/MG/CA/NA/CL/MN/FE
  across all 65,600 files). Not caused by s01 or `_prep_filters` -- those exclusions
  PROTECT waters (`_prep_filters.single_carbon_het_resindices` excludes HOH) and spare
  monatomic ions deliberately (same function's docstring: "Monatomic ions -- metals,
  halides, LU in 1plu -- are single-atom residues too and must survive"); s02/
  prepwizard behavior is assumed, not verified. Leading suspect:
  `consolidated_BioLiP2_split` splits metals into separate ligand entries. Verify against
  a source file before assuming a fix. This corrupts protonation states everywhere and
  means metal-coordination vdGs can't exist in the current library. Raising trim radius
  does not fix this. Deferred to the next parent DB.
- s01_trim_database.py config hazards for the next real run (the trim radius itself is
  DONE -- `:57` is now 20 A with the shell rationale at :50-56):
    * `:44-45` `origin_dir`/`target_dir` still point at `/home/sophia/DockDesign/...`.
    * `:48` `overwrite_pdbs = False`. This no longer silently writes nothing --
      `utils.set_up_outdir:46-52` raises on a non-empty target and :187 does per-file
      resume skipping -- but a fresh `target_dir` is still required.
    * `:49` `skip_to_output_pdbs = True` with `:47` pointing at
      `20240809_database_dict.json`: a rerun would skip dict rebuilding and reload a
      2024 dict at :158.
- FIXED IN CODE, NOT YET IN DATA: prepwizard orphan-backbone-N artifact.
  `_prep_filters.incomplete_backbone_resindices` (:22) drops AA residues missing any
  of N/CA/C (2y1x SAH A:1001, 1c53 GLY A:3) and is wired into both pre-prepwizard paths
  (`s01_trim_database.py:217`, `_protonate_pdbs.py:34-35,123-127`). It cannot repair
  `prepwizard_BioLiP2`, which is already prepped -- hence the ~461 dropped environments
  in the 2026-09-06 run, which are detected and skipped downstream by the duplicate-
  atom-name guard in `vdg_miner/programs/fingerprints_to_pdbs.py:100-131`.
- FIXED IN CODE, NOT YET IN DATA: two-character chain IDs written across PDB columns
  21-22 (`ASPA4  66` = chain A4) collide with the single column every reader uses (ProDy,
  vdG-miner line hashing and ligand scan). 670 structures in prepwizard_BioLiP2 have such
  chains (103 with a real residue merge;
  `scratch/chain_fix/twochar_chain_pdbs.txt`), and every one was dropped whole by the
  2026-09-06 build. Verified on 8any: 0 environments before, 42 nr vdGs (ATP/NAD CGs)
  after. The same segments are also written as HETATM in the source files (113,720
  standard amino acids on two-char chains; none on one-char chains), so a chain remap
  alone turns protein into "ligands" (8any: 59 TRP-indole CGs). s01 now applies both
  text-level fixes before ProDy parses (`_chain_ids.remap_chain_ids`,
  `_prep_filters.embedded_amino_acid_hetatm_to_atom`, both via
  `_prep_filters.text_repairs:396-410`), records the remap as REMARK 900 lines, and skips
  structures with > 62 chains (1bos, 8evs; `CHAIN_POOL` is 62 chars, so the limit and the
  pool agree by construction). `scripts/remap_chain_ids.py --all` repairs an existing
  database into a new dir -- this is what produced prepwizard_BioLiP2_repaired. Parsing
  first would bake the merge in silently: ProDy 2.6 gives PHE 4 66 + ASP A4 66 one
  resindex. Left as HETATM after repair (correct): 10 free amino-acid ligands with OXT
  and no peptide bond (6suv GLU 104 x7, 8esw LYS, 7tgh TYR, 8hl4 GLU).
- FIXED IN CODE, NOT YET IN DATA: modified residues without prepwizard (audit
  2026-09-09, `scratch/modres_audit/results/`): genuinely renamed modres = 3322
  residues, 1686 within 4.5 A of a ligand (352 canonical-part contact, 426
  modification-only, 908 both); MSE renamed to MET = 4881 residues, 1148 near a ligand in
  1137 structures; non-canonical amino acids left in chains = 85 residues, 15 near a
  ligand (10 SEC); covalent cofactors (PLP/HEM/HEC/FAD) are HETATM and mined as ligands
  today. s01 now applies `_prep_filters.modified_residues_to_protein` (:314-395) before
  parsing: MSE->MET and SEC->CYS (`_ALTERNATE_HEAVY_ATOMS` in
  `functions/vdg_struct_utils.py:316` admits SE for both), then every other chain-bonded
  HETATM residue whose CCD type is `*PEPTIDE LINKING` becomes an ATOM record under its
  own resname (neither slot nor ligand). The CCD type, not backbone atom names, is the
  discriminator (:335): 353 chain-bonded cofactor residues (SAM/SAH/NXL/0G6) carry N/CA/C
  names. `resources/ccd_polymer_types.tsv` exists (2026-09-09, from
  `scripts/fetch_ccd_polymer_types.py`, login node -- compute nodes have no network);
  without it s01 warns and only the renames happen. Cost: ~1.7k slots that are `X` today,
  all unreachable by hit finding. `X` stays as a safety net. STILL OPEN: verified on
  prepwizard output only (SEC in 5jsy/2wpn/6z9g -> ATOM CYS with SE); the raw BioLiP
  input is on another machine, so the first real exercise of the MSE/KCX/LLP paths is the
  next s01 run -- spot-check a few outputs.

AFTER THE 2026-09-07 VALIDATION RUN (library otherwise sound, zero invariant
violations)
- The CG-connectivity check misses geminal-bonded perception errors. Renamed since this
  was written: `_cg_atoms_are_bonded` is now `_cg_bond_components`
  (`clus_and_deduplicate_vdgs.py:172-198`, `MAX_CG_BOND_DIST = 2.5` at :169, used at
  :1001). The rewrite catches "two well-formed halves sitting far apart" via connected
  components, but it is still purely distance-based on the CG coordinates and never
  consults the SMARTS bond list, so a geminal (1-3) pair at ~2.2-2.5 A still passes.
  ~831/2.2M rows affected, 74 fragments. The docstring (:165-168) scopes the check to
  "gross perception failures, not refereeing bond geometry" -- so either add a separate
  SMARTS-bonded-pair check or decide the gap is acceptable and say so there.
- ~544 rows have deposited-geometry bond lengths too short to be real (modeling errors,
  not a pipeline bug). No minimum-bond-length screen exists anywhere. Decide whether to
  screen these out.

AFTER THIS REBUILD -- REQUIRED INPUT TO THE NEXT ONE
- Contact-gate threshold (`sasa.MIN_CONTACT_AREA`, ratified at 0.0) was chosen against
  divergence nulls at mirror scale. Once a library exists, re-decide it on geometric
  recurrence -- per contact-area band (0-0.25, 0.25-0.5, 0.5-1, 1-2, 2-5, >5 A^2), the
  histogram of `cluster_num_parents` (distinct biounits; never `cluster_size`, which NCS
  inflates) and `cluster_pose_radius`. Tight clusters recurring across unrelated biounits
  = real interaction geometry; diffuse singletons = packing noise, so raise the threshold
  above that band next version. Cross-check the pi classes separately. Also re-run the
  CG-scoped sweep once the subgraph vocabulary exists: smaller fragments have less
  buriable surface, which makes any absolute threshold more binding. Full rationale:
  ~/docking/scratch/claude_plan_notes/decision_records.md, "DR-3 CLOSED".
- Cation-pi and S-pi to PROTEIN rings are a known limitation of the buried-SASA
  criterion, not a radius problem: 96% / 94% of the missed pairs are inside the 6.5 A
  prefilter with zero credited area, because the ligand-side moiety is a single atom
  whose sphere is mostly buried by its own molecule. Widening the prefilter buys ~4%.
  Decide whether these need a non-SASA term.

DESIGN DECISIONS (no urgent deadline)
- Protonation strategy: matching is currently charge-loose (library keys pool variants
  via `charge_normalized_fragment`/`group_protonation_variants`,
  `extract_fragment_smiles.py:109,152`); decide whether to make it charge-strict for
  donor/acceptor accuracy, or keep pooling and rely on the read-path warning
  (`hit_finder_core._warn_charge_only_miss:425`) for charged-key misses.
- Statistical-support denominator: NOW HAS DIRECT EVIDENCE, not just theory. In the
  2026-09-10 contact-gate analysis, apparent chemical enrichment across contact-area
  bands PASSED a burial/distance-matched null but FAILED a structure-level permutation
  null, i.e. the signal rode on shared ancestry (NCS copies, homologs, redeposits), and
  a multinomial null that treated those as independent draws was 2.7x too permissive.
  Any library-wide statistic compared against a null must permute at the parent-biounit
  level. See ~/docking/scratch/claude_plan_notes/decision_records.md, DR-3 23:1x entry.
- Statistical-support denominator: `identify_bioisosteres/common.py:162,180` and
  `hit_finder_core.py:966,988` still use raw cluster/observation counts;
  `cluster_num_parents` is written (`clus_and_deduplicate_vdgs.py:446`) but read only by
  tests. Decide which to report.
- Nested fragments (`cccn` inside `cccnc`) share observations, so counts across fragments
  are correlated -- same issue as the overlapping-sites entry in pitfalls.md, but
  systematic once enumeration moves from balls to all connected subgraphs. Buckets are
  independent, so clustering is unaffected; normalize only when aggregating across
  fragments (bioisosteres, hit scoring).
- Calibrate Stage-1 RMSD cutoff `T` against recall on real mined environments (not stored
  nr vdGs). Blocks a real answer on the Stage-2 flanking-context question below.
- Stage 2 splits pose clusters by flanking context; unclear if that's a better use of
  representative budget than a tighter `T`. Needs the `T` calibration first.
- Query/library key reachability: build an explicit relaxation map (protonation, H-count)
  instead of the ad hoc `resolve_fragment_alias` + charge-only-miss warning. Ring-size
  differences should stay hard constraints (they change CG geometry) and already are
  (`Frags.ring_query_for_atom:170`).
- Aromatic ring-size gate (pyridine/pyrrole, pyrimidine/imidazole currently pool, because
  `Frags.py:202-203` returns no ring primitive for aromatic atoms). The measurement the
  old bullet asked for is now in the docstring at `Frags.py:175-180` (divergence only
  2-3x null; fused-aromatic shattering cost). Decide from that rather than re-measuring.
- Fragment-selection cutoff (`--min-instances 250`, `extract_fragment_smiles.py:84`,
  `make_sge_scripts_for_frags.py:371`) is a judgment call, sensitive to sampling noise
  near the boundary (~40% membership churn across five seeds at constant set size,
  quantified at `extract_fragment_smiles.py:386-390`), and ring annotation splits one
  chemical fragment's count across several keys (may be excluding real fragments like
  boronic acids). `resolve_include_fragments` can force a fragment past the threshold.
  Revisit before next build.
- Decide how to treat icodes. De facto policy already in code: residues with >1 distinct
  icode are skipped on both sides (`dock_utils.py:47-49`,
  `align_and_cluster.py:1005-1011`; a lone icode is fine).
- `resolve_fragment_alias` (`vdg_npz_utils.py:983`) -- keep and document. NOT test-only,
  as an earlier version of this bullet claimed: it is called in production by
  `identify_bioisosteres/compare_cg_geometries.py:40,154`, so deleting it would break
  bioisostere comparison.
- `_has_deliberate_charge_assignment` (`utils.py:110`, called at :184): only fragment that
  would exercise it is below the selection threshold. Delete, or lower the threshold.

WHEN ADDING NEW LIGANDS / REBUILDING THE LIGAND FILE
- `UNDESIRED_ELEMENTS` (`fragment_database_ligs.py:499-507`) is a denylist and already
  leaks (12 CCD elements are neither organic nor denylisted); a carbon-bearing complex of
  one would be mined as an organic fragment. Switch to an allowlist
  (C,N,O,S,P,F,Cl,Br,I,B) before adding non-CCD ligands. Se is already decided -- it is
  denylisted (:502); B is deliberately NOT denylisted (:509, boronic acids and boronate
  esters are real covalent warheads).
- Remove cryoprotectants in `fragment_database_ligs.py` -- no resname skip list exists
  there. The only nearby filter is much narrower and downstream:
  `extract_fragment_smiles.is_solvent_artifact:52-69` covers halogen oxyanions only.
- Hydrogens: no longer needed for contacts (`sasa.py:8` -- heavy atoms only, H is neither
  surface nor occluder) and deliberately absent from keys (`ligand_perception.py:171-180`
  deletes them because both toolkits count explicit H in the SMARTS `D` primitive;
  `hit_finder_core.py:405-410` raises on a query mol with graph H). But H is still
  load-bearing in one place: `ligand_perception.py:182` deletes them AFTER
  `PerceiveBondOrders`, which needs the positions. The CCD-template path doesn't care
  (H counts come from the template, :280-283), so the open question is narrower than it
  was: a new NON-CCD ligand has no template, lands on the OpenBabel fallback, and does
  still need H. Decide how to build them for that case.
- Consider a `keys_only` fast path in `fragment_database_ligs.py` that skips the
  permutation/site-grouping machinery (unneeded when only the element string is used) --
  verified no-op on CCD output, worth doing for a rebuild. Does not exist today.

